  "aboutDmxS2Title": "2) DMX frame — break, start code, channels",
  "aboutDmxS2PillBreak": "Break",
  "aboutDmxS2PillStartCode": "Start code",
  "aboutDmxS2PillSlots512": "512 slots",
  "aboutDmxS2PillRefresh": "Refresh",
  "aboutDmxS2Intro": "A DMX frame is sent in a continuous loop. It starts with a “break” (line held low), then a start code, then up to 512 values (0–255). Each device reads only the channels it needs (based on its address and mode).",
  "aboutDmxS2HowToReadTitle": "Reading a frame (simple view)",
  "aboutDmxS2HowToReadB1": "Break + MAB: marks the start of the frame (re-sync).",
  "aboutDmxS2HowToReadB2": "Start code: most of the time 0x00 (lighting data).",
  "aboutDmxS2HowToReadB3": "Slots 1→512: each slot = 1 channel (0–255).",
  "aboutDmxS2HowToReadB4": "The frame repeats continuously: new values overwrite old ones.",
  "aboutDmxS2PracticalTitle": "In practice",
  "aboutDmxS2PracticalB1": "A 16-bit mode uses 2 channels for one function (e.g., Pan coarse + Pan fine).",
  "aboutDmxS2PracticalB2": "The more universes you send, the higher the overall load (console/nodes/network).",
  "aboutDmxS2PracticalB3": "If you see flicker, check cabling/termination/topology first before blaming the console.",
  "aboutDmxS3Title": "3) DMX wiring — RS-485, topology, pinout",
  "aboutDmxS3PillRs485": "RS-485",
  "aboutDmxS3PillDaisyChain": "Daisy-chain",
  "aboutDmxS3PillNoY": "No Y-split",
  "aboutDmxS3Pill120ohm": "120 Ω",
  "aboutDmxS3Intro": "DMX512 is based on RS-485: a differential bus that needs clean lines. Golden rule: a daisy-chain from source to fixtures, and a terminator at the end.",
  "aboutDmxS3TopologyTitle": "Recommended topology",
  "aboutDmxS3TopologyB1": "Source → Fixture 1 → Fixture 2 → … → last fixture.",
  "aboutDmxS3TopologyB2": "Avoid Y-splits: they create reflections (instability).",
  "aboutDmxS3TopologyB3": "If you need branches: use an opto-isolated DMX splitter.",
  "aboutDmxS3PinoutTitle": "Connector reminder",
  "aboutDmxS3CablesTitle": "Cables: what actually works",
  "aboutDmxS3CablesB1": "Use proper DMX cable (~120 Ω), not random mic cable (except short emergency).",
  "aboutDmxS3CablesB2": "Keep lengths reasonable and avoid tight coils near power/dimmers.",
  "aboutDmxS3CablesB3": "Bad cable can “work” until it doesn’t (humidity, length, load).",
  "aboutDmxS4Title": "4) Termination & splitters — bus stability",
  "aboutDmxS4Intro": "On an RS-485 bus, termination prevents reflections at the end of the line. Splitters (opto-isolated) create clean branches and protect the source.",
  "aboutDmxS4TerminatorTitle": "Termination (120 Ω): when and how",
  "aboutDmxS4TerminatorB1": "Add a terminator on the last device (or an XLR terminator plug).",
  "aboutDmxS4TerminatorB2": "One terminator per line (not everywhere).",
  "aboutDmxS4TerminatorB3": "Important on long runs, heavy loads, electrically noisy environments.",
  "aboutDmxS4SplittersTitle": "Splitters / opto-isolation",
  "aboutDmxS4SplittersB1": "Lets you build 2/4/8 branches without Y-splits.",
  "aboutDmxS4SplittersB2": "Electrical isolation reduces ground loops and protects console/node.",
  "aboutDmxS4SplittersB3": "Useful to re-drive the signal over long distances.",
  "aboutDmxS5Title": "5) Troubleshooting — a simple method (fast and reliable)",
  "aboutDmxS5Intro": "When a fixture does not respond, isolate the problem: address, mode, DMX line, or the fixture itself. Run a short checklist before rebuilding the patch.",
  "aboutDmxS5QuickChecksTitle": "Quick checklist",
  "aboutDmxS5QuickB1": "Correct DMX address? (and not 001 vs 000 depending on display).",
  "aboutDmxS5QuickB2": "Correct DMX mode (channel count)?",
  "aboutDmxS5QuickB3": "Correct universe/output (console, node, port)?",
  "aboutDmxS5QuickB4": "Test with a known-good cable, replacing one element at a time.",
  "aboutDmxS5QuickB5": "Add/remove termination at the end of the line and observe the change.",
  "aboutDmxS5IfFlickerTitle": "If it flickers / drops out",
  "aboutDmxS5IfFlickerB1": "Look for a hidden Y-split, wrong splitter, or a dodgy XLR adaptor.",
  "aboutDmxS5IfFlickerB2": "Keep DMX away from power/dimmers and avoid huge cable loops.",
  "aboutDmxS5IfFlickerB3": "Reduce complexity: 1 universe, 1 line, 1 fixture, then rebuild.",
  "aboutDmxS5GoldenRuleTitle": "Golden rule",
  "aboutDmxS5GoldenRuleBody": "You should be able to run a single fixture on a single line. If this base test fails, the issue is not “the full patch”: it’s addressing, cabling, or hardware.",
  "aboutDmxS6Title": "6) Art-Net — DMX over IP (vocabulary and traps)",
  "aboutDmxS6PillUdp": "UDP",
  "aboutDmxS6PillNodes": "Nodes",
  "aboutDmxS6PillBroadcast": "Broadcast",
  "aboutDmxS6PillUnicast": "Unicast",
  "aboutDmxS6Intro": "Art-Net carries DMX universes over Ethernet (UDP). A console/software sends universes on the network, and a node converts them to physical DMX outputs.",
  "aboutDmxS6BasicsTitle": "Key points",
  "aboutDmxS6BasicsB1": "A node = network ↔ DMX (multiple ports).",
  "aboutDmxS6BasicsB2": "Broadcast sends to everyone (easy but can saturate).",
  "aboutDmxS6BasicsB3": "Unicast sends only to a target node (often more stable on big rigs).",
  "aboutDmxS6BasicsB4": "Watch universe indexing: Art-Net is often 0-based (DMX Universe 1 = Art-Net 0).",
  "aboutDmxS6WhenTitle": "When to choose Art-Net?",
  "aboutDmxS6WhenB1": "Small/medium rigs, quick setups, lots of compatible gear.",
  "aboutDmxS6WhenB2": "Great if the network is clean (proper switch/cables, no Wi-Fi).",
  "aboutDmxS6WhenB3": "If it saturates: move to unicast and segment the network (VLAN / dedicated LAN).",
  "aboutDmxS7Title": "7) sACN (E1.31) — multicast, priorities, clean networking",
  "aboutDmxS7PillE131": "E1.31",
  "aboutDmxS7PillMulticast": "Multicast",
  "aboutDmxS7PillPriority": "Priority",
  "aboutDmxS7PillIgmp": "IGMP",
  "aboutDmxS7Intro": "sACN (ANSI E1.31) is a modern standard to carry DMX universes over IP. It is widely used in large systems thanks to multicast and priority handling.",
  "aboutDmxS7KeyIdeasTitle": "Key ideas",
  "aboutDmxS7KeyIdeasB1": "Multicast: each universe is a network “group” (nodes subscribe).",
  "aboutDmxS7KeyIdeasB2": "IGMP snooping on switches becomes essential at scale (otherwise you flood the LAN).",
  "aboutDmxS7KeyIdeasB3": "Priority: multiple sources can exist; the highest priority wins.",
  "aboutDmxS7KeyIdeasB4": "Just like Art-Net: clean network + decent switches/cables = stability.",
  "aboutDmxS8Title": "8) DMX vs Art-Net vs sACN — summary (what to pick?)",
  "aboutDmxS8Intro": "All three ultimately control channels. The difference is distribution (DMX cable vs network), scale, and robustness.",
  "aboutDmxS8QuickTableTitle": "4-line summary",
  "aboutDmxS8QuickB1": "DMX: simple, robust, 1 cable = 1 line (512 channels).",
  "aboutDmxS8QuickB2": "Art-Net: very common, easy to deploy, watch broadcast.",
  "aboutDmxS8QuickB3": "sACN: more “large-system friendly”, multicast + priorities, requires a well configured network.",
  "aboutDmxS8QuickB4": "In all cases: documentation + incremental testing saves a lot of time.",
  "aboutDmxS8ChooseTitle": "Fast choice",
  "aboutDmxS8ChooseB1": "Small setup: direct DMX or Art-Net (simple).",
  "aboutDmxS8ChooseB2": "Many universes: dedicated network + nodes; Art-Net unicast or sACN.",
  "aboutDmxS8ChooseB3": "Very large rigs/infrastructure: sACN + properly managed IGMP switches.",
